\section*{Exact ball-packing criterion in symplectic dimensions at least six}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Higher-dimensional ball packing}: For symplectic dimension $2n$ with $n\ge3$, the announced criterion for packing closed balls inside a larger open ball is the strict volume inequality together with each strict pairwise capacity inequality. Embeddings must be defined around the closed source balls.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Higher-dimensional ball packing}: Explicit geometric models place nearly all volume over positive-genus surfaces and provide concave area profiles. A one-handle comparison and commutator-based local deformation realize the required surface packings. The models are treated in the relevant geometric cases, with limiting area profiles controlling the passage to the closed-ball packing conditions.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Higher-dimensional ball packing}: For symplectic dimension $2n$ with $n\ge3$, the announced criterion for packing closed balls inside a larger open ball is the strict volume inequality together with each strict pairwise capacity inequality. Embeddings must be defined around the closed source balls. The parameters, regularity assumptions and normalization in this question are retained in applying that assertion.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 343 describes Lean coverage for the closed-ball packing equivalence and its necessary capacity inequalities.

\textit{Higher-dimensional ball packing}: The manuscript links \texttt{BallPacking.lean}, \texttt{BallPackingNecessity.lean}.

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

